# SSH client policy — the TRANSPORT half of this repo's remote-access story.
# Symlinked to ~/.ssh/config by scripts/link-dots.sh. OS-neutral: nothing here branches on
# macOS vs WSL, so there is no config.mac / config.wsl split.
#
# THE SPLIT, and why it is not negotiable. This repo is PUBLIC. A host inventory is
# HostName / Port / User — a map of the tailnet and who to log in as — so it lives in an
# UNTRACKED ~/.ssh/config.local, exactly as per-machine git identity lives in ~/.local-gitconfig
# (see git/gitconfig). What stays here is POLICY: options that are argued from measurement and
# are worth carrying to any machine that clones this repo. Host ALIASES appear here because they
# are already all over this repo's tracked files (mise.toml, scripts/wsl-reclaim.ts); the
# addresses behind them are not.
#
# ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING. ssh takes the FIRST value it obtains for any option and never revises
# it, so this file reads most-specific -> least-specific, and the Include comes first so a
# machine-local decision always outranks a house default. A missing config.local is NOT an
# error (verified: ssh -G still resolves, exit 0), so a fresh clone works before the local file
# exists — you simply have no hosts defined yet.

Include ~/.ssh/config.local

# --- bandwidth: compress before the bytes reach a slow uplink -----------------------------
# r99 is a compute box on an ASYMMETRIC line. Measured 2026-09-09: egress caps at ~1.3 MB/s
# (~10.4 Mbps) while ingress reaches 145 Mbps — 40x. A remote TUI sends keystrokes UP the fast
# path and receives redraws DOWN the slow one, which is exactly why `herdr --remote r99-wsl`
# felt laggy while every latency metric read clean: RTT 33ms, 0% loss over 240 packets, ssh
# round-trip p99 146ms with zero samples over 500ms in 60 trials.
#
# It is genuinely the link, not a tuning problem: 4 parallel streams measured SLOWER than one
# (0.48 vs 0.72 MB/s). A TCP window limit would have summed HIGHER; degradation under
# parallelism is a saturated bottleneck. So the only remaining lever is sending fewer bytes.
#
# Terminal data is ~10x compressible (a 2,225,104-byte `ls -laR` dump gzips to 225,552).
# Measured A/B across this link with the same payload:
#     Compression=no   6.47s / 8.02s
#     Compression=yes  0.84s / 0.89s      <- ~8x
# Client-side only: `ssh -v` shows `compression: zlib@openssh.com` negotiated in BOTH
# directions against a stock sshd, so nothing is required on the server.
#
# This reaches herdr WITHOUT changing how herdr is launched. `herdr --remote` writes its own ssh
# config, and that file's first line is `Include "~/.ssh/config"` — so with first-value-wins,
# a Host block here outranks herdr's own `Host *` defaults. Verified through herdr's generated
# config: `ssh -F <herdr's config> -G r99-wsl` reports `compression yes`.
# NOTE: ControlPersist keeps an existing master alive, so an already-running `herdr --remote`
# must be restarted before it picks this up.
#
# COST, so the tradeoff is visible rather than folded away: CPU on both ends (both had headroom
# — host 53%, guest loadavg ~2), and a small LOSS on already-compressed payloads. Compression is
# therefore scoped to this slow link, never `Host *`. For a bulk transfer of compressed data:
#     ssh -o Compression=no ...
Host r99-wsl r99
    Compression yes

# --- keepalive: prevent an idle NAT/firewall from silently reaping a live connection -------
# Probe every 30s, give up after 3 misses (~90s), so a long-lived agent session notices a dead
# link and reconnects promptly instead of hanging on a socket the path has already forgotten.
# TCPKeepAlive is off deliberately: it is the kernel-level, spoofable probe, and the
# application-level ServerAlive* pair above is both encrypted and the one that actually drives
# ssh's own disconnect decision.
Host *
    ServerAliveInterval 30
    ServerAliveCountMax 3
    TCPKeepAlive no
